\subsection{Activation Functions}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item \textbf{Step (threshold) function} \hfill\textit{(hard decision)}
\[
f(\text{net}) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if net} \ge 0 \\ 0 & \text{if net} < 0 \end{cases}
\]
\item \textbf{Sigmoid function} \hfill\textit{(smooth 0 to 1 output)}
\[
f(\text{net}) = \dfrac{1}{1+e^{-\text{net}}}
\]
\end{enumerate}

A perceptron has weights $w_1=0.6$, $w_2=0.6$, bias $b=-0.5$, and step activation. Compute its output for $x_1=1, x_2=0$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{net} &= (0.6)(1) + (0.6)(0) + (-0.5) = 0.1 \\
y &= f(0.1) = 1 \quad (\text{since } 0.1 \ge 0)
\end{align*}
\boxed{y = 1}

Using the same perceptron as Example 2.19, compute the output for $x_1=0, x_2=0$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{net} &= (0.6)(0) + (0.6)(0) + (-0.5) = -0.5 \\
y &= f(-0.5) = 0 \quad (\text{since } -0.5 < 0)
\end{align*}
\boxed{y = 0}

Using the same perceptron as Example 2.19, compute the output for $x_1=1, x_2=1$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{net} &= (0.6)(1) + (0.6)(1) + (-0.5) = 0.7 \\
y &= f(0.7) = 1 \quad (\text{since } 0.7 \ge 0)
\end{align*}
\boxed{y = 1}

\textbf{Observation from Examples 2.19 to 2.21:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item The output is $1$ whenever at least one input is $1$, and $0$ only when both inputs are $0$
\item This perceptron has learned the logical \textbf{OR} function
\item A single perceptron can only represent \textbf{linearly separable} functions (like AND, OR); it cannot represent XOR, which is why hidden layers are needed (Section 2.3.3)
\end{enumerate}

A perceptron has weights $w_1=0.4$, $w_2=0.3$, bias $b=-0.2$, and sigmoid activation. Compute its output for $x_1=2, x_2=1$. (Use $e^{-0.9} \approx 0.4066$.)

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{net} &= (0.4)(2) + (0.3)(1) + (-0.2) = 0.8+0.3-0.2 = 0.9 \\
y &= \frac{1}{1+e^{-0.9}} = \frac{1}{1+0.4066} = \frac{1}{1.4066}
\end{align*}
\boxed{y \approx 0.711}

A perceptron has weights $w_1=0.5$, $w_2=-0.5$, bias $b=0.3$, and sigmoid activation. Compute its output for $x_1=0, x_2=2$. (Use $e^{0.7} \approx 2.0138$.)

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{net} &= (0.5)(0) + (-0.5)(2) + 0.3 = 0 - 1 + 0.3 = -0.7 \\
y &= \frac{1}{1+e^{0.7}} = \frac{1}{1+2.0138} = \frac{1}{3.0138}
\end{align*}
\boxed{y \approx 0.332}

A perceptron has weights $w_1=-0.2$, $w_2=0.6$, bias $b=0$, and sigmoid activation. Compute its output for $x_1=3, x_2=0$. (Use $e^{0.6} \approx 1.8221$.)

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{net} &= (-0.2)(3) + (0.6)(0) + 0 = -0.6 \\
y &= \frac{1}{1+e^{0.6}} = \frac{1}{1+1.8221} = \frac{1}{2.8221}
\end{align*}
\boxed{y \approx 0.354}

\textbf{Step vs Sigmoid:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item Step activation gives a hard $0$ or $1$ output only
\item Sigmoid activation gives a smooth value strictly between $0$ and $1$, useful as a "confidence" score
\item As net $\to +\infty$, sigmoid $\to 1$; as net $\to -\infty$, sigmoid $\to 0$
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Multilayer Neural Networks and Forward Propagation}

A multilayer perceptron is a network with one or more \textbf{hidden layers} placed between the input layer and the output layer. Every neuron in one layer connects to every neuron in the next layer, each connection has its own weight, and information flows forward from input to output through the hidden neurons.

