\textbf{Signs of Overfitting:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item Training accuracy is very high (often near 100\%) while testing accuracy is much lower
\item The gap between training and testing accuracy keeps growing as the model grows more complex
\item The tree has very deep branches or leaves covering only one or two training examples
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Pruning}

Before pruning a subtree at node $X$, validation accuracy is 82\%. After replacing the subtree at $X$ with a leaf, validation accuracy becomes 85\%. Should node $X$'s subtree be pruned?

\textbf{Solution:}
Compare accuracies: $85\% > 82\%$, so accuracy does not decrease after pruning; in fact it improves.
\boxed{\text{Yes, prune the subtree at } X}

Before pruning a subtree at node $Y$, validation accuracy is 90\%. After replacing the subtree at $Y$ with a leaf, validation accuracy becomes 88\%. Should node $Y$'s subtree be pruned?

\textbf{Solution:}
Compare accuracies: $88\% < 90\%$, so accuracy decreases after pruning.
\boxed{\text{No, keep the subtree at } Y}

Before pruning a subtree at node $Z$, validation accuracy is 78\%. After replacing the subtree at $Z$ with a leaf, validation accuracy remains 78\%. Should node $Z$'s subtree be pruned?

\textbf{Solution:}
Compare accuracies: $78\% = 78\%$, accuracy does not decrease. Reduced Error Pruning prefers the simpler tree whenever accuracy does not get worse.
\boxed{\text{Yes, prune the subtree at } Z}

A confusion matrix is a table that summarizes a classifier's predictions against the true labels for a binary classification problem. It has four counts: \textbf{True Positives (TP)}, \textbf{False Positives (FP)}, \textbf{False Negatives (FN)}, and \textbf{True Negatives (TN)}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cc|cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{Actual}} \\
& & Positive & Negative \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{\textbf{Predicted}} & Positive & TP & FP \\
& Negative & FN & TN \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Accuracy, Precision, Recall, and F1-Score}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item $\text{Accuracy} = \dfrac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN}$ \hfill\textit{(overall correctness)}
\item $\text{Precision} = \dfrac{TP}{TP+FP}$ \hfill\textit{(of predicted positives, how many were right)}
\item $\text{Recall} = \dfrac{TP}{TP+FN}$ \hfill\textit{(of actual positives, how many were found)}
\item $\text{F1-score} = \dfrac{2 \times \text{Precision} \times \text{Recall}}{\text{Precision}+\text{Recall}}$ \hfill\textit{(harmonic mean of Precision and Recall)}
\end{enumerate}

A classifier gives the confusion matrix below.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cc|cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Actual} \\
& & Positive & Negative \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{Predicted} & Positive & 40 & 10 \\
& Negative & 5 & 45 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Compute Accuracy, Precision, Recall, and F1-score.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{Accuracy} &= \frac{40+45}{40+45+10+5} = \frac{85}{100} = 0.85 \\
\text{Precision} &= \frac{40}{40+10} = \frac{40}{50} = 0.8 \\
\text{Recall} &= \frac{40}{40+5} = \frac{40}{45} \approx 0.889 \\
\text{F1} &= \frac{2(0.8)(0.889)}{0.8+0.889} = \frac{1.422}{1.689}
\end{align*}
\boxed{\text{Accuracy}=0.85,\ \text{Precision}=0.8,\ \text{Recall}\approx0.889,\ F1\approx0.842}

